% TM-100 PDF page 14, printed folio 12. Section 3.2 (1) Stream. Figure 3.5 "Class
% relations of class 'stream'" (a diagram, reproduced as printed) and the start of
% Figure 3.6 "Part of class 'stream'" (caption on PDF p15).
% Transcribed by eye from 300dpi crops.
% Prose (PDF p13-14): "A stream consists of two queues; a queue of processes waiting
% for events (in the case of stream, for an object being put) and a queue of objects
% which are waiting to be taken out by processes.  Class 'waiting_queue' defines queues
% of the first.  This queue is constructed on a doubly-linked list, and frames
% (entries) in the queue must be instances of class 'waiting_frame'.  Class 'process'
% inherits class 'waiting_frame'.  Class 'post_queue' defines queues of the second.
% Any class of objects, including integers, atoms, and strings, can be put into the
% queue.  A stream has a 'post_queue' slot which has an instance of class 'post_queue',
% rather than it inherits class 'post_queue'.  It is because a channel, which is a
% variation of stream, will have a post queue for messages different from that of a
% stream.  Class 'channel' will inherit class 'stream' with overriding its
% 'post_queue' slot (see 3.2 (2)). ... A stream also has a 'waiting_queue' slot ...
% we chose to have the slot because of some subtle problem when initiating a stream."
% [transcriber] ":add_object(Stream!post_queue, Object)" has no trailing comma in the
% print (as printed).

% ---- Figure 3.5 (diagram) ----
                    waiting_queue   post_queue
                          ^         ^
                       has:         :has
                          :         :
                          stream..:
                Figure 3.5  Class relations of class 'stream'

% ---- Figure 3.6 ----
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
    class  stream  has

    :create(Class, Stream) :-
            :new(Class, Stream);

    instance

    attribute ( waiting_queue   := Queue :- :create(#waiting_queue, Queue) ),
              ( post_queue      := Queue :- :create(#post_queue, Queue) );

    :put(Stream, Object):-
            :inhibit_interrupt(#process),
            :add_object(Stream!post_queue, Object)
            (   :empty(Stream!waiting_queue), !,
            ;   :unhook(Stream!waiting_queue, Process),
                :resume(Process, stream(Stream)) ),
            :allow_interrupt(#process);

    :get(Stream, Object):-
            :inhibit_interrupt(#process),
            (   :remove_object(Stream!post_queue, Object), !
